{"id":"dell-earnings-fq2-fy2027","title":"DELL earnings — Dell Technologies, fiscal Q2 FY2027","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-09-01","html":"<p>Management sees AI server orders running well ahead of what it can ship and raised every full-year target, and the thing that could spoil it is the rising cost of memory.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-09-01. Next report: 2026-11-24 (Webull; not confirmed elsewhere). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<p><strong>This report is about 10 weeks old and is replaced on Nov 24, 2026. Read it as the bar the next one has to clear.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. AI server orders $60.9B in the quarter; backlog $95B.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised. Fiscal 2027 revenue $192.0B from $167.0B; AI servers $74B from $60B; non-GAAP EPS $25.50 from $17.90. Fiscal Q3 revenue $49.0B at the midpoint.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> memory cost inflation, and whether customers will keep paying for it.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> no lean. It is above the 200-day ($340.20) but far under everything else. A close under $340.20 turns it down; a close above $421.84 turns it up.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $46.97B (up 58%) vs $44.50B estimate, and above the $44.0B–$45.0B guide. AI server revenue $16.4B (up 100%).</li><li>GAAP EPS $6.34 vs $4.40 estimate; non-GAAP EPS $7.04. The estimate is Webull's and analysts usually estimate non-GAAP, so that EPS gap mixes the two. Treat it as rough.</li><li>Gross margin 20.9%. Part of the revenue growth is higher costs passed through in prices, not more boxes.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> fiscal Q3 revenue $49.0B at the midpoint; GAAP EPS $6.10; non-GAAP EPS $6.50. This is what Nov 24 gets measured against.</li><li><strong>Orders and backlog:</strong> $60.9B of AI server orders and &quot;exited the quarter with a record $95 billion backlog.&quot; Jeff Clarke, Vice Chairman and COO. The pipeline grew again.</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> demand is above supply in servers and storage, with no date given for relief. Memory is the main shortage.</li><li><strong>Costs:</strong> the 10-Q expects component cost inflation to last through the fiscal year.</li><li><strong>Customers:</strong> large buyers are ordering further ahead to lock in supply. Cost-sensitive buyers are putting off PC upgrades; PC units are likely to fall in the second half.</li><li><strong>Margins:</strong> gross profit dollars are expected to grow while the margin rate is squeezed by the shift to AI servers.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Memory and component shortages</strong>, and whether customers can absorb the price rises. Inflation expected through the fiscal year.</li><li><strong>Inventory and commitments:</strong> inventory doubled to $21.3B since year-end; purchase commitments much larger.</li><li><strong>Customer financing:</strong> $7.5B of originations in the quarter against $2.4B a year ago. Dell is lending more to get the sale.</li><li><strong>Uneven timing of AI revenue:</strong> a few large deployments move a quarter.</li><li><strong>PC demand:</strong> units likely to fall in the second half as buyers delay.</li><li><strong>Tariffs and geopolitical events</strong> (10-Q).</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-memory-cost-inflation-both-ways\">The big worry: memory cost inflation, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Memory price gains slowing. Micron expects more moderate increases after its November quarter. TrendForce (Oct 20) sees DRAM contract prices up about 20% this quarter, after about 40% and about 80% in the two quarters before.</li><li>Price ceilings in long-term contracts kicking in. TrendForce names these as one reason gains are slowing; Micron says most of its 26 long-term deals carry floor and ceiling prices.</li><li>Customers continuing to accept pass-through. Large buyers ordering further ahead says they care about supply more than price.</li><li>New memory plants. Micron's come online between 2027 and 2030, so this is a later help, not a near one.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Supply getting tighter, as Micron expects for 2027 and 2028. Its management says it cannot see when DRAM supply and demand balance.</li><li>More capacity moving to AI memory. TrendForce (Oct 2, via Evertiq) says PC memory supply could shrink as capacity shifts to servers, and that PC brands are already cutting storage in mainstream models.</li><li>Storage costs joining in: the same report has NAND up 15%–20% this quarter, with enterprise SSD the one category where price gains are speeding up.</li><li>Customers pushing back. The Beige Book says firms' margins are squeezed as buyers resist price increases, and Dell's own cost-sensitive buyers are already delaying.</li><li>Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom all named memory as a margin drag and guided margin about a point lower. Dell sits downstream of all of them.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Better than Super Micro: Dell keeps about 21 cents of gross profit per sales dollar against about 12, and it has passed costs through so far.</li><li>It bought ahead: inventory of $21.3B is a cushion if prices keep rising and a risk if they fall.</li><li>It is carrying more weight to do it: debt principal $34.7B, and its financing arm is lending three times what it did a year ago. A hiking Fed (about 90% odds for December, per Fed Desk) raises the cost of both.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Buybacks and dividend:</strong> $4.3B returned in the quarter ($3.8B buybacks, $405M dividends). Dividend $0.63 a quarter. About $10.4B of buyback authorization left.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> principal $34.7B after $2.5B of new notes.</li><li><strong>Working capital:</strong> inventory $21.3B; customer financing originations $7.5B in the quarter.</li><li><strong>Capital spending and hiring:</strong> no forward spending guide found. The 10-Q says costs will be held down through reorganizations and limits on outside hiring.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Michael Dell, founder, Chairman and CEO. He ran Dell Inc. from 1984 to 2004 and again from January 2007. One person holds both jobs.</li><li><strong>Number two:</strong> Jeff Clarke, Vice Chairman and COO, age 63 per the March 2026 10-K. He also took charge of the PC unit in 2025 (CRN Asia).</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> David Kennedy, since September 2025; a Dell employee since 1998. He came in as interim after Yvonne McGill left by mutual agreement; the March 2026 10-K lists him as CFO without interim. The chief accounting officer also changed weeks before that (CRN Asia).</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found. The 10-K says Dell is highly dependent on Michael Dell and also relies on Clarke.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> yes, firmly. Class A and B shares carry ten votes, Class C (the one you can buy) carries one. Michael Dell's group and Silver Lake together held about 91.7% of the votes and 53.8% of the shares as of Mar 9, 2026. Michael Dell's Class A shares alone are a majority of votes. Dell is a controlled company under NYSE rules, though it keeps a majority-independent board.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> the board proposed moving the company's legal home from Delaware to Texas, voted at the June 25, 2026 meeting, effective about July 1 if approved (outcome not found). The proxy material says Michael Dell and Silver Lake had the votes to ensure approval. Investigations: none found. The legal proceedings note of the 10-Q was not read.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> a lot, by the company's own words, and outside holders cannot outvote him. The cushion is a deep bench of long-serving executives. The gap is that the two men at the top are 61 and 63 with no named successor.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"DELL\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"DELL · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">DELL · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"259.8\" y2=\"283.2\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"266.2\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"2.5\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"258.4\" y2=\"315.3\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"263.8\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"23.7\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"268.2\" y2=\"301.1\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"293.0\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"8.0\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"24.2\" x2=\"24.2\" y1=\"273.5\" y2=\"304.2\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"22.8\" y=\"276.7\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"25.6\" 